description:
Combine this milestone's rules into one simulation of `LoadLibraryExW` that loads a DLL **and all of its dependencies**, and reports where each one came from.

```cpp
struct World
{
    std::string appDir;
    std::string currentDir;
    std::vector<std::string> pathDirs;
    std::vector<std::string> userDirs;                          // added with AddDllDirectory
    bool defaultDirsSet{ false };                               // SetDefaultDllDirectories(LOAD_LIBRARY_SEARCH_DEFAULT_DIRS) was called
    std::set<std::string> knownDlls;
    std::map<std::string, std::vector<std::string>> files;     // full path -> the DLL names it imports
};

std::vector<std::string> loadLibraryEx(const World& w, const std::string& file, unsigned flags);
```

- `file` is either a full path (starts with a drive letter and `:\`) or a bare name.
- The result lists every module loaded, dependencies first (as in lesson 1.02), each as `name -> full path`, then ends with `ok`. If any module cannot be found, the result is instead the single line `missing <name>` for the first one that fails, in load order.
- Each module is loaded once; a dependency already loaded earlier in this call is reused (rule "already loaded").

**Finding a file.** A full path loads that exact file if it exists. For a bare name:

1. Already loaded in this call: reuse it.
2. A known DLL: `C:\Windows\System32\<name>`, no search.
3. Otherwise search the folders, in order:
   - if the call's `flags` contain any `LOAD_LIBRARY_SEARCH_*` value, **or** `defaultDirsSet` is true: the folders from lesson 4.02's drill (the flags of this call, plus `LOAD_LIBRARY_SEARCH_DEFAULT_DIRS` when `defaultDirsSet` and the call has none of its own), where the "DLL load dir" is the folder of the **top** DLL being loaded, and only applies if it was loaded by full path;
   - otherwise the standard order from lesson 4.01: application folder, `C:\Windows\System32`, `C:\Windows\System`, `C:\Windows`, current directory, PATH folders.

The flag values are those of lesson 4.02's drill. A folder and a name are joined with one backslash.

